---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: 'How do I convert Mbps to MB/s?',
    answer:
      'Divide megabits per second by eight to estimate megabytes per second. The conversion works because one byte contains eight bits.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many MB/s is 100 Mbps?',
    answer:
      'At the full line rate, 100 Mbps is 12.5 MB/s. Real download programs can show less because of network overhead, server speed, Wi-Fi, and other activity.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does Steam or my browser show MB/s?',
    answer:
      'Internet plans usually describe megabits per second, while file transfers often show megabytes per second. The units measure the same data using different-sized units, so divide Mbps by eight to compare.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does a 5 GB download take at 100 Mbps?',
    answer:
      'Using decimal units and the full link rate, the math gives about 6 minutes 40 seconds. At 90% efficiency, the estimate is about 7 minutes 24 seconds.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is MB the same as MiB?',
    answer:
      'No. A megabyte is 1,000,000 bytes in decimal units, while a mebibyte is 1,048,576 bytes in binary units. Check the exact unit shown by the program.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why can a real download take longer than this estimate?',
    answer:
      'The formula assumes the file source can send at that rate and your connection sustains it. Protocol overhead, Wi-Fi, congestion, device storage, and other people using the network can lower the achieved rate.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: questions.map(({ question, answer }) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: answer },
  })),
};

const calculatorSchema = {
  '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'Mbps to MB/s converter and download-time calculator',
  url: 'https://guessthespeed.com/mbps-to-mbs/',
  applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: 0, priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  author: { '@id': 'https://guessthespeed.com/#author' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://guessthespeed.com/#author' },
};
---

<Base
  title="Mbps to MB/s Converter (and Download Time Calculator)"
  description="Convert Mbps to MB/s and Gbps, then estimate a file download time at full speed and with a realistic 90% efficiency."
  breadcrumb="Mbps to MB/s"
  schema={[faqSchema, calculatorSchema]}
>
  <main class="wrap content-page">
    <span class="round-banner">Bits, bytes, and download time</span>
    <h1>Mbps to MB/s Converter</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Divide Mbps by eight to estimate MB/s: a 100 Mbps connection has a line-rate equivalent of
      12.5 MB/s. Enter either unit below to see the other value and its Gbps equivalent.
    </p>
    <article class="content-card stk">
      <h2>How many megabytes per second is your speed?</h2>
      <section class="converter-panel stk" data-mbps-converter aria-label="Mbps and MB/s converter">
        <div class="converter-grid">
          <label class="converter-field">
            <span>Internet speed in Mbps</span>
            <input
              class="converter-input"
              data-mbps-value
              type="number"
              min="0"
              step="any"
              inputmode="decimal"
              value="100"
              aria-describedby="converter-hint"
            />
          </label>
          <label class="converter-field">
            <span>File transfer speed in MB/s</span>
            <input
              class="converter-input"
              data-mbps-file-value
              type="number"
              min="0"
              step="any"
              inputmode="decimal"
              value="12.5"
              aria-describedby="converter-hint"
            />
          </label>
        </div>
        <p id="converter-hint" class="converter-hint">
          Type a positive number into either field. One byte contains eight bits.
        </p>
        <p class="converter-output" data-conversion-output aria-live="polite">
          100 Mbps equals 12.5 MB/s or 0.1 Gbps.
        </p>
        <div class="converter-gigabit stk">
          <span>Equivalent internet speed</span>
          <output data-gbps-output aria-live="polite">0.1 Gbps</output>
        </div>
      </section>

      <h2>Why does Steam or a browser show MB/s?</h2>
      <p>
        Internet plans label bandwidth in megabits per second. A browser, game launcher, or
        download manager may report the number of megabytes moving each second. Eight bits fit
        in one byte, so divide the plan's Mbps by eight to estimate a line-rate MB/s. The speed
        shown in an app is a transfer rate, not a different internet plan.
      </p>
      <p>
        I use a pizza to remember it: picture one byte as a pizza cut into eight slices. Mbps
        counts slices per second; MB/s counts whole pizzas per second. For the longer version,
        see <a href="/internet-speed-101/">Internet Speed 101</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How long will a file download take?</h2>
      <p>
        Enter a file size and a connection speed to compare an idealized full-rate transfer with
        a more realistic estimate at 90% efficiency. The calculator treats MB and GB as decimal
        units, which makes the line-rate math easy to compare with internet plan speeds.
      </p>
      <section
        class="download-panel stk"
        data-download-calculator
        aria-label="Download time calculator"
      >
        <div class="download-grid">
          <label class="converter-field">
            <span>File size</span>
            <input
              class="converter-input"
              data-file-size
              type="number"
              min="0"
              step="any"
              inputmode="decimal"
              value="5"
              aria-describedby="download-hint"
            />
          </label>
          <label class="converter-field">
            <span>Size unit</span>
            <select class="converter-select" data-file-unit aria-describedby="download-hint">
              <option value="MB">MB</option>
              <option value="GB" selected>GB</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="converter-field">
            <span>Internet speed in Mbps</span>
            <input
              class="converter-input"
              data-download-speed
              type="number"
              min="0"
              step="any"
              inputmode="decimal"
              value="100"
              aria-describedby="download-hint"
            />
          </label>
        </div>
        <p id="download-hint" class="converter-hint">
          At 100 Mbps, a 5 GB decimal file takes about 6 minutes 40 seconds at full rate.
        </p>
        <div class="download-results">
          <p>At 100% efficiency: <output data-time-full aria-live="polite">About 6 min 40 s</output></p>
          <p>At 90% efficiency: <output data-time-realistic aria-live="polite">About 7 min 24 s</output></p>
        </div>
        <p class="converter-output" data-download-summary aria-live="polite">
          5 GB at 100 Mbps takes about 6 min 40 s at 100% efficiency, or about 7 min 24 s at 90%.
        </p>
      </section>

      <h2>What is the difference between MB and MiB?</h2>
      <p>
        This calculator uses decimal file sizes: one MB is 1,000,000 bytes and one GB is
        1,000,000,000 bytes. A MiB is 1,048,576 bytes and a GiB is 1,073,741,824 bytes. These
        binary prefixes are useful when a computer program counts in powers of two; the unit
        label tells you which number it means. The
        <a href="https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Units/binary.html">NIST guide to binary prefixes</a>{' '}
        lists the decimal and binary definitions.
      </p>

      <h2>Which Mbps speeds are useful to compare?</h2>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Mbps to megabytes per second conversions" tabindex="0">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <caption>Line-rate conversion using one byte per eight bits</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Internet speed</th><th scope="col">Equivalent file rate</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">10 Mbps</th><td>1.25 MB/s</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">25 Mbps</th><td>3.125 MB/s</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">50 Mbps</th><td>6.25 MB/s</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">100 Mbps</th><td>12.5 MB/s</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">300 Mbps</th><td>37.5 MB/s</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">500 Mbps</th><td>62.5 MB/s</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">1,000 Mbps</th><td>125 MB/s</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="guide-table-note">
        These are idealized decimal conversions. A real file transfer can be slower because the
        source server, route, Wi-Fi, protocol overhead, and other network activity all matter.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {questions.map(({ question, answer }) => (
          <section>
            <h3>{question}</h3>
            <p>{answer}</p>
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        Need a household plan estimate too? Try the
        <a href="/how-much-internet-speed-do-i-need/">household speed calculator</a>, then
        <a href="/what-is-a-good-internet-speed/">read what makes a speed good</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="story-cta">
        <a class="go" href="/">Start a game</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>

<script>
  import '../scripts/convert-client';
</script>

<style>
  .converter-panel,
  .download-panel {
    margin: 24px 0;
    padding: clamp(16px, 3vw, 28px);
    background: var(--soft);
  }

  .converter-grid,
  .download-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
  }

  .download-grid {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(110px, 0.5fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  .converter-field {
    display: grid;
    min-width: 0;
    gap: 8px;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  .converter-input,
  .converter-select {
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 54px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border: 2.5px solid var(--ink);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  .converter-hint {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .converter-output {
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    color: var(--ink) !important;
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  .converter-gigabit {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-top: 18px;
    padding: 14px;
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .converter-gigabit span {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  .converter-gigabit output {
    font-family: "Bricolage Grotesque Variable", sans-serif;
    font-size: 26px;
    font-weight: 900;
  }

  .download-results {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 18px;
  }

  .download-results p {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 14px;
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  .download-results output {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 6px;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: "Bricolage Grotesque Variable", sans-serif;
    font-size: 22px;
    font-weight: 900;
  }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .converter-grid,
    .download-grid,
    .download-results {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .converter-panel,
    .download-panel {
      padding: 14px;
    }
  }
</style>
